<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayutthaya Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ayutthaya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ayutthaya Kingdom was a Siamese state founded in 1351 in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. It expanded at the expense of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a> and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> in the fifteenth century. Its rise is closely associated with the Khmer Empire's decline and the shift of its centre southwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom.jpg" alt="Ayutthaya Kingdom" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xiengyod · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Thailand_(Ayutthaya_period).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ayutthaya emerged in the late 13th and 14th centuries from a mandala of four port <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of the lower Chao Phraya valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, Lavo, Phrip Phri and Suphannaphum. In one scholar's account the merging polities were Lopburi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suphanburi">Suphanburi</a>, Ayodhya and Phetchaburi, each of which had sent its own missions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Uthong V held Phetchaburi and Ayodhya by descent and gained Suphannabhum and Lavo through marriage, and 4 March 1351 is conventionally given as the foundation of the kingdom. Persian merchants knew the new centre as shahr-i naw, the new city. The name Ayutthaya comes from the Sanskrit Ayodhya, by way of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakien">Ramakien</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early kingdom was a maritime confederation, closer to the Malay trading polities than to inland states such as Sukhothai. It sent forces down the peninsula and drew tribute from the Malay principalities as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temasek">Temasek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>. Its early chronology is probably fabricated by later elites, since early Ayutthaya kept no records. The 1430s to 1600 were an age of rising warfare, in which Ayutthaya fought for Tavoy, Mergui, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> and Martaban. From 1569 to 1584 it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Toungoo Burma, then regained independence.</p><p>After the Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a>, Asian traders used portage routes across the peninsula that Ayutthaya controlled, and it became an intermediary between the great empires of the early modern world. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a> (r. 1657–1688) Persian and then European influence was strong, and an embassy went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> in 1686.</p><p>Chinese trade kept the economy lively after the French and English left in 1688. Failure to settle the succession and the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a> weakened the elite and the labour control on which its army and administration rested. The Konbaung dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> invaded in 1759–1760 and 1765–1767. After a 14-month siege the city fell and was destroyed in April 1767, ending the 417-year-old kingdom. Within fifteen years the seat of authority had moved to Thonburi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Ayutthaya followed the mandala system, in which the capital's control weakened with distance, and provincial governors were never fully brought to heel. The reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat">Borommatrailokkanat</a> (r. 1448–1488) placed the king at the centre of a stratified hierarchy, and the king was regarded as the incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>. Ramathibodi II (r. 1491–1529) required every freeman to register as a phrai under a lord, owing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> labour and military service. The state ran on a small service nobility, with the rest of the population serfs. The main religion was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> rituals conducted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests. The court developed the classical dramatic forms khon and lakhon from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakien">Ramakien</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ayutthaya Kingdom” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Khmer influence extended over present-day Thailand, Laos and southern Vietnam and was felt in later states such as <b>Ayutthaya</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Alaungpaya then subdued Manipur in 1758 and invaded Siam, but in 1760 he fell ill during the siege of <b>Ayutthaya</b> and died on the march home.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He fought a long war with <b>Ayutthaya</b> under King Borommatrailokkanat, who moved his own residence to Phitsanulok in the north to direct the struggle; the fighting over the borderlands of the former Sukhothai kingdom lasted, with interruptions, from the 1450s to the 1470s and ended without decisive change.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Lan Xang was landlocked, and its exports travelled by river and overland trails to <b>Ayutthaya</b>, Cambodia, Vietnam and Yunnan: gold, benzoin, lac, ivory, musk and other forest goods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Singapura grew rich in the 14th century, alarming <b>Ayutthaya</b> to the north and Majapahit to the south, and after at least two invasions it was sacked by one of them in 1398.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rattanakosin Kingdom grew out of the wreckage left by the fall of <b>Ayutthaya</b> to the Burmese Konbaung dynasty in 1767.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
